      {
        "id": 145381,
        "name": "Arcanist Lupus",
        "username": "Arcanist_Lupus",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/arcanist_lupus/{size}/2169_2.png",
        "created_at": "2015-07-24T21:49:35.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"Pydro\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/pydro/48/1844_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Pydro:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I hate it when fantasy books use \"normal\" names, but just change the spelling. Or when they use the exact same concept but change the name, just to change the name.</p>\n<p>It doesn't take 3 months to do, it takes 3 moons.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>It's all about creating the feel. &nbsp;Consistant spelling has only existed for a few hundred years, and having a character named 'Samuel' or 'Mary' in a fantasy world without monotheism is linguistically absurd anyways. &nbsp;So giving characters fancily spelled names is a translation convention saying 'these characters come from a culture that is similar to, but not the same as, the culture that produced those names on Earth'. &nbsp;I'd argue that in some cases it's the most accurate way of naming characters in cultures inspired by Earth cultures.</p>",
        "post_number": 22,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T04:23:08.107Z",
