      {
        "id": 145384,
        "name": "BlueHairedMeerkat",
        "username": "BlueHairedMeerkat",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/bluehairedmeerkat/{size}/1967_2.png",
        "created_at": "2015-07-26T16:37:09.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"Silverleaf\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/silverleaf/48/2332_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Silverleaf:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<div> But for me I hate it when there's a special themed name for something that already has a well-known generic and intuitive name. MtG's graveyard for trash/discard pile, library for hand/deck/whatever the hell library means, crystals for mana/magic points/spell points. Sure, they add flavour, but at the cost of making me have to remember what they mean every single time they come up, and I'd much rather be giving my attention to the actual game rather than learning another \"language\". </div>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>I like Netrunner. Netrunner is a game I enjoy. But&nbsp;<em>gods</em>, is it guilty of this. Not only do hand, deck and discard pile have fancy names, but they have&nbsp;<em>different&nbsp;</em>names depending on which side you'e playing! So the Runner has heap, stack and grip, while the Corporation has R&amp;D, Archives, and HQ. It drives me&nbsp;<em>mad</em>.</p>\n<p>[/rant]</p>",
        "post_number": 25,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T04:23:08.323Z",
