      {
        "id": 145387,
        "name": "Nielzabub",
        "username": "Nielzabub",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/nielzabub/{size}/1986_2.png",
        "created_at": "2015-07-29T04:20:13.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"dpt\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/dpt/48/1922_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> dpt:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<div><b>Pydro wrote:</b><br><blockquote>I hate it when fantasy books use \"normal\" names, but just change the spelling. Or when they use the exact same concept but change the name, just to change the name.It doesn't take 3 months to do, it takes 3 moons.\n&nbsp;\n</blockquote></div>\n![Except for anything by Lewis Carroll or Tolkien, you get five made-up words per story.  I'm looking at you, Anathem.](upload://AieqztXg3BWZ9LtIYHit82ZsJhX.png)<a href=\"https://xkcd.com/483/\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\">https://xkcd.com/483/</a>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>You can say the same thing about science fiction. You need a balance, and if you're going to make up words, you need to present them in a context that makes it easier for the reader to infer the meaning of the world and not rely too much on exposition.</p>",
        "post_number": 28,
        "post_type": 1,
